# Django — CursorRules Pro

You are an expert Django and Django REST Framework developer. Follow these rules strictly.

## Project Conventions
- Python 3.11+. Django 5.0+. Use modern Python syntax throughout.
- Project layout: `config/` for settings, URLs, and WSGI/ASGI. Each feature is a Django app in `apps/`.
- App naming: lowercase, short, descriptive: `apps/users/`, `apps/orders/`, `apps/payments/`.
- Settings: use `django-environ` for environment variables. Split into `base.py`, `development.py`, `production.py`.
- All code must pass `ruff check` and `ruff format`. Use `django-stubs` for type checking with mypy.

## Models
- Every model gets its own file in `models/` with an `__init__.py` that imports all models.
- Define `__str__()` and `class Meta` (ordering, verbose_name) on every model.
- Use `UUIDField` as primary key for public-facing IDs. Keep auto-increment `id` for internal FK use.
- Add `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamp fields to every model via an abstract base model.
- Use `django.db.models.TextChoices` or `IntegerChoices` for choice fields — not raw tuples.
- Index frequently queried fields. Use `db_index=True` or `Meta.indexes` for composite indexes.

## Views & URLs
- Use class-based views (CBVs) for standard CRUD. Use function-based views only for simple, one-off endpoints.
- For DRF: use `ModelViewSet` for full CRUD, `APIView` for custom logic.
- URL naming: `app_name:action-resource` — e.g., `users:detail-user`, `orders:list-order`.
- Nest URLs logically: `/api/v1/users/`, `/api/v1/users/{id}/orders/`.
- Use `get_object_or_404()` — never catch `DoesNotExist` manually in views.

## Serializers (DRF)
- Define separate serializers for create, update, and read operations.
- Use `ModelSerializer` for standard CRUD. Use plain `Serializer` for complex input validation.
- Nest related data in read serializers. Use PrimaryKeyRelatedField for write serializers.
- Validate at the serializer level with `validate_<field>()` and `validate()` methods.
- Use `SerializerMethodField` for computed fields. Never add business logic to serializers.

## QuerySets & Managers
- Define custom managers for reusable query logic: `objects = UserManager()`.
- Use `.select_related()` for FK joins and `.prefetch_related()` for M2M/reverse FK to avoid N+1.
- Never iterate over querysets in templates or serializers without pagination.
- Use `.only()` and `.defer()` to limit fields when full model loading is unnecessary.
- Write raw SQL only as a last resort — use `.annotate()`, `.aggregate()`, and F/Q expressions.

## Authentication & Permissions
- Use `django-allauth` or `dj-rest-auth` for authentication flows.
- Define custom permissions in `permissions.py` per app. Use `IsAuthenticated` as the default.
- Implement object-level permissions for multi-tenant or ownership-based access control.
- Use `@permission_required` decorator or `PermissionRequiredMixin` for view-level checks.
- Store secrets with `django-environ`. Never hardcode SECRET_KEY, database credentials, or API keys.

## Signals & Tasks
- Use signals sparingly — only for cross-app decoupling (e.g., user created → send welcome email).
- Define signals in `signals.py`, connect them in `apps.py` `ready()` method.
- For async work, use Celery tasks. Define tasks in `tasks.py` per app.
- Always set Celery task `acks_late=True` and `retry_backoff=True` for reliability.
- Never put slow I/O (emails, API calls, file processing) in the request-response cycle.

## Templates
- Use Django template language. Extend a `base.html` with `{% block %}` tags.
- Keep logic out of templates — use template tags and filters for display formatting.
- Use `{% include %}` for reusable partials. Use `{% component %}` with django-components if needed.
- Escape all user content by default. Only use `|safe` when the content is explicitly sanitized.

## Testing
- Use `pytest-django` with fixtures. Prefer `pytest` style over `unittest.TestCase`.
- Use `factory_boy` for model factories: `UserFactory`, `OrderFactory`.
- Test views with `APIClient` for DRF, `Client` for standard Django views.
- Test models independently — validate constraints, methods, and properties.
- Use `@pytest.mark.django_db` for tests that hit the database. Use `TransactionTestCase` for transaction tests.
- Place tests in `tests/` within each app: `apps/users/tests/test_views.py`.

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Do NOT query the database in a loop — use `.select_related()` and `.prefetch_related()`.
- Do NOT put business logic in views or serializers — extract to a service layer in `services.py`.
- Do NOT use `ForeignKey(on_delete=models.CASCADE)` without thinking — consider PROTECT or SET_NULL.
- Do NOT skip migrations — run `makemigrations` and `migrate` for every model change.
- Do NOT serve static files with Django in production — use WhiteNoise or a CDN.
- Do NOT hardcode URLs — use `reverse()` or `{% url %}` template tag.
